{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"update.json\", \"summary\": \"Requested a new experiment for the VerifyLeader question (533b966489544f45b4fcf14f6edce57f-question-0) that supplies the end-to-end consequence its account still lacks. Five real nodes run as three voters and two non-voters with a recording state machine; the leader commits v1, is partitioned from the other two voters while the non-voters stay reachable, verifies itself immediately, and then the two remaining voters elect a successor and commit v2. The old leader verifies again and its state machine is read, so the harness measures whether a verified read could ever be served after the real quorum moved on. A local probe produced the bounded outcome: verification succeeded in 0 ms after the cut, the successor committed v2 at about 2 s, and by then the old leader had stepped down and verification failed with not-the-leader, so stale_verified_read was false; the control, which also cuts the non-voters, failed verification at lease expiry. Four comparisons cover the trigger's expected success, the post-failover refusal, the absence of a verified stale read, and the control's failure. Two probe defects were corrected before submission, an inverted partition condition and a misused log accessor; the harness now runs both cases cleanly in about ten seconds. The submission validates with zero schema errors and requests one execution.\"}"}}
